        <p class="profile-detail-region">Movement · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Meiji Restoration</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Where</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>: Edo (renamed Tōkyō in 1868) and Kyōto; the domains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>, Chōshū, Tosa, Hizen and Mito; the treaty ports; and Hokkaidō, where the Republic of Ezo was ended.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">The opening of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Perry</a> Expedition and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unequal-treaties">unequal treaties</a> (1853–1858); political violence and the shishi loyalists (1858–1864); the anti-Tokugawa alliance and domain rebellion (1864–1867); the shōgun’s resignation (1867) and the restoration of 3 January 1868; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> (1868–1869); the abolition of the domains (1868–1873) and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> stipends (1876); the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> (1889).</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Leading figures</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-yoshinobu">Tokugawa Yoshinobu</a>, the 15th and last Tokugawa shōgun; the court noble Iwakura Tomomi; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori">Saigō Takamori</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okubo-toshimichi">Ōkubo Toshimichi</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kido-takayoshi">Kido Takayoshi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/takasugi-shinsaku">Takasugi Shinsaku</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a> of Chōshū; Sakamoto Ryōma of Tosa, who brokered the Satsuma–Chōshū Alliance; the teacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoshida-shoin">Yoshida Shōin</a>; Katsu Kaishū, who negotiated for the Tokugawa.</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Outcome</span><span class="info-stat-val">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shogun">shogunate</a> and feudalism ended; the 302 domains became 72 prefectures in 1872; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> privileges were abolished and conscription introduced in 1873; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> industrialised and was a military power by 1895; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution">Meiji Constitution</a> of 1889 lasted until the Allied occupation after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</span></div>
        </div>
