    <section class="kid-tale" aria-labelledby="kid-tale-title">
      <h2 class="kid-tale-title" id="kid-tale-title">The story of the Meiji Restoration</h2>
      <ol class="kid-tale-pages"><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate.jpg" alt="Tokugawa Shogunate" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Tokugawa Shogunate <span class="kid-tale-cr">Maproom, CC BY-SA 3.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 170 years ago, Japan was governed by the shōguns of the Tokugawa family, and lords ran their own lands.</p><p>Many people went hungry, and samurai, who were paid in rice, fell into debt.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dejima.jpg" alt="Dejima" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Dejima <span class="kid-tale-cr">Isaac Titsingh, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>For a long time Japan was closed, and only Dutch traders could come, at a place called Dejima.</p><p>Then American warships led by Matthew Perry arrived and made Japan open two ports.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/samurai.jpg" alt="Samurai" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Samurai <span class="kid-tale-cr">Unknown author, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>Some young samurai, called shishi or &quot;men of spirit&quot;, wanted to give power back to the Emperor.</p><p>Two strong regions, Satsuma and Chōshū, joined together against the shōgun.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji.jpg" alt="Emperor Meiji" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Emperor Meiji <span class="kid-tale-cr">Uchida Kuichi, Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>More than 150 years ago, the teenage Emperor Meiji declared that power was his again.</p><p>The last shōgun had already given up his power, but a war was fought before his side gave in.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saigo-takamori.jpg" alt="Saigo Takamori" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Saigo Takamori <span class="kid-tale-cr">C. Nakagawa (from signature), Public domain</span></figcaption></figure><p>The new government ended the samurai class, and every man now had to serve in the army.</p><p>Some samurai, led by Saigō Takamori, rebelled, but the new army defeated them.</p></li><li><figure><img src="/images/encyclopedia/meiji-constitution.jpg" alt="Meiji Constitution" loading="lazy" width="320" height="220"><figcaption>Meiji Constitution <span class="kid-tale-cr">Kantei, CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure><p>Japan built schools, factories and shipyards, and sold silk to pay for machines.</p><p>Its new constitution, a set of rules for running the country, lasted until after the Second World War.</p></li></ol>
    </section>

    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Economic strain, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthew-c-perry">Perry</a> Expedition and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unequal-treaties">unequal treaties</a> that followed undermined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-shogunate">Tokugawa shogunate</a>, and many lower and middle-ranking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> became shishi, “men of spirit”, devoted to the Emperor.</li><li>Led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satsuma-domain">Satsuma</a> and Chōshū, an alliance of domains overthrew the shogunal system; on 3 January 1868 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-meiji">Emperor Meiji</a> declared political power restored to the Imperial House, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/boshin-war">Boshin War</a> ended Tokugawa resistance.</li><li>The new government abolished the domains and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samurai">samurai</a> class, built schools, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conscription">conscript</a> army and industries, and laid the foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Sakamoto Ryōma first went to Katsu Kaishū intending to assassinate him for his pro-foreign views; instead Katsu won him over to his plan for rearmament, and Sakamoto followed him to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kobe">Kobe</a> Naval Training Centre.</p></aside>
    </section>

